    "text": "## Cylinder head\n\nDuring development of the aluminium cylinder head, priority was given to enhanced use of the exhaust gas energy to ensure a fast engine warm-up phase.\n\n#### Technical features\n\n- 4-valve technology \n\n- Cross-flow cooling \n\n- Integrated exhaust manifold \n\n- - Designed for alternative fuels\n\n#### Design\n\nThe cross-flow cylinder head allows the coolant to flow from the inlet side to the exhaust side via the combustion chambers. It is divided into two sections above and below the exhaust manifold. It flows through several ports, absorbing the heat. It flows from the cylinder head into the thermostat housing, mixing with the remaining coolant. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nExhaust side Intake side\nCylinder head\nExhaust port\nUpper cooling area\nIntegrated exhaust manifold\nLower cooling area s511_147\nIntegrated exhaust manifold\nThe four exhaust ports within the cylinder head have been combined into one central flange in the integrated\nexhaust manifold. The exhaust gas turbocharger is bolted directly onto this flange.\nThis design has several benefits:\n- -\nThe coolant is warmed up by the exhaust gas while When the system is operating at full capacity, the\nthe engine is warming up. The engine reaches its exhaust manifold and the exhaust gas are cooled\noperating temperature faster. This reduces fuel even more, and the engine can be operated in a\nconsumption, and the vehicle interior can be larger consumption-optimised and exhaust-\nheated sooner. optimised lambda=1 range when operating at full\n- Due to the smaller exhaust-side wall surface capacity.\nextending to the catalytic converter, the exhaust\ngas looses little heat during the warm-up phase.\nThis allows the catalytic converter to heat up to\noperating temperature faster, despite the cooling\neffect of the coolant.\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n16",
