    "text": "## Variable valve timing\n\nAll EA211 engines employ stepless inlet camshaft adjustment, engines with an output of 103 kW or more have stepless exhaust camshaft adjustment as well. \n\nThe camshaft adjuster directly on the camshafts carries out the adjustment according to the engine load and speed. They are adjusted by the valves for the camshaft adjustment, which are integrated directly in the oil circuit. The two Hall senders are used to identify the adjustment angle. \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nHall sender G300 (exhaust side)\nCamshaft control valve 1 N205 (inlet side)\nHall sender G40\n(inlet side)\nExhaust camshaft control valve 1\nN318\nEnd cover for exhaust\ncamshaft adjuster\nPlug for inlet camshaft\nadjuster\ns511_149\nCamshaft adjustment versions\nEngine version Inlet camshaft adjustment Exhaust camshaft adjustment\n1.0 l 44/50/55 kW MPI engine Stepless up to a crank angle of 40° –\n1.2 l 63/77 kW TSI engine and\nStepless up to a crank angle of 50° –\n1.4 l 90 kW TSI engine\n1.4 l 103 kW TSI engine\nwith/without Active Cylinder Stepless up to a crank angle of 50° Stepless up to a crank angle of 40°\nManagement\nCamshaft adjuster seals and attachment\nTo ensure no engine oil finds its way onto the toothed belt, the camshaft adjusters are sealed. This is done by a\nrubber seal on the end cover of the exhaust camshaft adjuster and one on the plug for the inlet camshaft adjuster.\nBoth camshaft adjusters are attached using securing bolts on the camshafts.\nBoth bolts have a right-handed thread.\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n19\n\nEngine mechanics",
