    "text": "### Inlet and exhaust camshaft adjustment\n\nThe basic design of both camshaft adjusters is identical.\n\n#### Special features of the camshaft adjusters\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nVane-type adjusters\nThe camshaft adjusters function according to the Housing\nvane-type adjuster principle. Depending on which of\nthe two chambers the oil is guided to, the rotor turns\nand the camshaft turns with it. Chamber 2\nThe adjustment is stepless.\nRotor\nGear wheel\ns511_222\nExhaust camshaft adjuster return\nIf the engine is to start quickly, no residual gases may Exhaust camshaft adjuster\nenter the cylinders. To ensure this happens,\nReturn spring\nthe exhaust camshaft adjuster is locked in the “early\nposition” and the inlet camshaft adjuster is locked in\nthe “late position” when the engine is switched off.\nThe exhaust camshaft adjuster is set to a position\nwhich is opposite to the direction of engine rotation.\nDue to the large adjustment angle up to a 40° crank\ns511_223\nangle, the oil pressure alone may not be sufficient\nAdjustment direction for\nenough for this. “Engine off”\nA return spring on the exhaust camshaft adjuster\nassists the oil pressure when being set in the “early\nposition”.\nLocking\nWhen the engine is switched off, the exhaust Gear wheel\ncamshaft adjuster is locked in the early position and\nHousing\nthe inlet camshaft adjuster is locked in the late\nposition. This prevents adjustment of the camshafts Locking\nduring the engine start, and the engine starts faster. mechanism\nFurthermore, it prevents noises when the engine is\nstarting.\ns511_268\nRotor Return spring\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n20\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nValve timing\nThe use of an inlet and exhaust camshaft adjuster allows the valve timing to be optimized to the requirements of\nthe engine. This is because, depending on the engine's operation, different opening and closing times provide\nimportant advantages.\nValve timing for the exhaust valve Valve timing for the inlet valve\n40° in direction of late 50° in direction of early\nValve lift\n9 mm\nMax.\nMax.\nadjustment\nadjustment\nBasic position\nBasic position\nMax. valve\noverlap\n1 mm\nBDC TDC BDC\n��� ��� ��� ��� ��� ��� ��� ��� Crank angle [°]\nSpeed/ Pressure ratio Valve Effects\nload range intake manifold/ overlap\nexhaust system\nIdling Intake manifold None - Very few residual gases in the cylinder, producing quiet\npressure lower than the running.\nexhaust back pressure\nLow speed/ Intake manifold High - Residual gases are drawn out of the exhaust system into\nlow to medium load pressure lower than the the cylinders.\nexhaust back pressure The throttle valve is opened further to ensure there is an\nadequate supply of fresh air for the torque required.\nThe engine is dethrottled, the fuel consumption falls.\nLow speed/ Intake manifold High - Fresh air is forced into the cylinders, residual gases are\nhigh load pressure higher than forced out.\nthe exhaust back - Due to a low proportion of residual gas, the rated torque\npressure due to charge is reached at a low engine speed.\npressure - Improved response by the exhaust gas turbocharger\nand reduced tendency to knock.\nMedium engine Charge pressure Low - When pressure ratios are equal, a larger\nspeed/ approximately equal to valve overlap has little benefit.\nmedium load exhaust back pressure\nHigh speed/ Charge pressure lower Low - No residual gases forced back despite high exhaust back\nhigh load than the exhaust back pressure, preventing any disadvantageous mixture\npressure formation.\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n21\n\nEngine mechanics",
