    "text": "## Exhaust gas turbocharger\n\nAn exhaust gas turbocharger is used for charging in the TSI engines in the EA211 engine family. This was designed for a high torque at low engine speeds and a fast response. This allows the 1.4 l 103 kW TSI engine to reach its maximum torque of 250 Nm at just 1500 rpm. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nA special feature of the charge air system is its Charge air system\ncompact design. This means the exhaust gas Exhaust gas turbocharger\nturbocharger has to compress a smaller volume and\nthe required charge pressure is reached faster.\ns511_168\nExhaust gas turbocharger Charge air cooler\nEvery exhaust gas turbocharger was newly developed for their respective engine and the corresponding power.\nWhile the basic design with the air duct system, the lubrication or the cooling is identical in all versions, they differ\nin the dimensions of the turbine and compressor wheels. Another difference between them is found in the charge\npressure positioners. They can be replaced individually, however the attachment to the wastegate and the basic\nsetting after replacement differ according to the engine.\nFeatures of the exhaust gas turbocharger:\nSmall turbine and compressor wheel diameter with Wastegate attachment\ncorrespondingly low moments of mass inertia. Coolant return\nMaterial designed for a maximum exhaust gas Coolant supply\ntemperature of 950 °C.\nIntegration into the coolant circuit of the charge\nair cooling to keep the temperature at the shaft\nbearings low once the engine has been switched\noff.\n- Connection to the oil circuit for lubrication and for\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n- Connection to the oil circuit for lubrication and for cooling the shaft bearings. \n\n\n\n- Activation of the wastegate for charge pressure control by an electrical charge pressure positioner with integrated position sender. \n\ns511_225 Charge pressure Exhaust gas turbocharger positioner \n\nOil lines \n\n23\n\nEngine mechanics",
