    "text": "#### Functional sequence of the oil pressure regulation\n\nThe oil pressure level is set by the oil quantity being pumped. The amount of oil being pumped depends on the position of the slide unit, how far the two pump gears are apart, and the engine speed.\n\n##### Pressure build-up from engine start to approx. 1.8 bar\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\napprox. 1.8 bar to the oil circuit\nOnce the engine has started, the required oil pressure\nmust be built up as quickly as possible. The two pump to the oil pump\ngears are exactly opposite each other and the\nmaximum oil quantity at the engine speed is pumped a\ninto the oil circuit.\nValve for oil pressure\ncontrol (activated)\na) The valve for oil pressure control is activated by\nthe engine control unit using the earth and opens Control piston 1 + 2\nthe control port to chamber 2.\nb) The control piston is pressed onto the stop of the\nhigh pressure stage by the compression spring.\nCompression\nspring\nc) The oil pressure in chambers 3 and 4 totals less\nthan 1.8 bar and has no effect on the position of Pump gears\nthe slide unit. The compression spring presses the\nslide unit against the full flow stop.\nChamber 3 Chamber 4\nSlide unit\nCompression spring\nfull flow s511_249\n<1.8 bar\nEngine speed increases\nWhen the engine speed increases, the oil pump Chamber 2\npumps more oil and the oil pressure increases. At the Control piston Chamber 1\nsame time, the pressure in chambers 1 and 2 of the\ncontrol piston increases, and it is pushed to the left\nagainst the spring force. Because the pressure in\nchambers 3 and 4 of the slide unit still totals less than\n1.8 bar, the slide unit remains at the full flow stop.\nCompression spring s511_250\nto chamber 3 Return\nto chamber 4\nStop High pressure stage\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n30\n\n##### Low-pressure stage – approx. 1.8 bar\n\nAt approx. 1400 rpm, the oil pressure reaches the lower pressure stage of approx. 1.8 bar. This pressure is kept constant up to 400 rpm or 150 Nm respectively. \n\nWhen the engine speed increases, the oil flow quantity and the oil pressure increase; they decrease when the engine speed falls. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nOil pressure regulation when the oil pressure\nincreases to more than 1.8 bar\na) The valve for oil pressure control is activated by a\nthe engine control unit using the earth and opens Valve for oil\nthe control port to chamber 2. pressure control\n(activated)\nb) The increasing engine speed increases the\npressure in chambers 1 and 2 to more than 1.8 Chamber 2\nbar and the control piston is pushed to the left b\nagainst the spring force. The path from chamber 4 Return\nto the return to the sump is opened.\nChamber 3\nPump gears\nc) The pressure in chamber 3 increases to more than\n1.8 bar and pushes the slide unit slightly to the\nright, against the spring force. The oil from c\nchamber 4 is pressed back into the sump. The\npump gears no longer intermesh as much, and the s511_251\noil quantity being pumped, and therefore the oil Mesh range of the pump gears\npressure, decrease.\nLow-pressure stage (1.8 bar)\nOil pressure regulation when the oil pressure\nfalls below 1.8 bar\na) The valve for oil pressure control remains open. Control piston\nb) When the engine speed decreases, the pressure in Chamber 2\nchambers 1 and 2 falls below 1.8 bar and the\ncontrol piston is pushed to the right by the spring\nforce. The path from the oil circuit to chamber 4 of Chamber 3\nthe slide unit is opened.\nPump gears\nc) The pressure in chambers 3 and 4 is now equal\nagain. Together with the spring force, the slide unit c\nis pushed slightly to the left. The pump gears Slide unit Chamber 4\ncontinue to intermesh, and the oil quantity being s511_269\npumped, and therefore the oil pressure, increase.\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n31\n\nEngine mechanics\n\n##### Switching over to the high pressure stage – approx. 3.3 bar\n\nAt an engine speed of 4000 rpm or an engine load of 150 Nm, a switchover to the high pressure stage of approx. 3.3 bar occurs. To reach the higher pressure, the quantity of oil pumped is increased. \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nSwitchover position to the high pressure stage\na) The valve for oil pressure control is no longer\nControl port\nactivated by the engine control unit and closes the\na closed\ncontrol port to chamber 2.\nValve for oil\npressure control\nb) The lack of oil pressure in chamber 2 allows the (activated)\ncompression spring to push the control piston far Chamber 1\nto the right, and opens a large cross section to Chamber 2\nchamber 4. b\nReturn\nchamber 4\nc) The oil pressure in chamber 4 of the slide unit\nChamber 3\nincreases and pushes this far to the left together\nwith the compression spring. The two pump gears\nnow intermesh deeply and pump more oil, and the\noil pressure increases. c\nHigh-pressure stage (3.3 bar)\nSwitching back to the low pressure stage\nTo switch back to a low pressure stage, the valve for oil pressure control is activated again using the earth and it\nopens the control port to chamber 2. The oil pressure in chambers 1 and 2 pushes the control piston to the left\nagainst the spring force, closes the control port to chamber 4 and opens the return line to the sump. This causes the\noil pressure in chamber 4 to fall, and the slide unit is pushed to the right by the higher oil pressure in chamber 3.\nThe pump gears intermesh less, and the oil quantity being pumped, and therefore the oil pressure, decrease.\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n32\n\n##### High-pressure stage – approx. 3.3 bar\n\nAs with the low pressure stage, the oil pressure is also regulated at a constant 3.3 bar in the high pressure stage. When the engine speed increases, the oil quantity being pumped and the oil pressure also continue to increase. To keep the oil pressure at a constant 3.3 bar, the quantity of oil being pumped is adjusted. The regulation at a constant oil pressure is carried out the same way as it is for the low pressure stage. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nOil pressure regulation when the oil pressure\nincreases to more than 3.3 bar\na) The valve for oil pressure control is not activated\nControl port\nby the engine control unit and closes the control\na closed\nport to chamber 2. Valve for oil\npressure control Control\nb) The oil pressure in chamber 1 is now high enough (activated) piston Chamber 1\nthat it pushes the control piston to the left against\nChamber 2\nthe force of the spring, and opens the return port\nfrom chamber 4 to the sump. b\nc) The pressure in chamber 4 falls, and the slide unit Chamber 3 Return\nis pushed to the right against the compression\nspring by the high oil pressure in chamber 3.\nThe pump gears no longer intermesh as deeply,\npumping less oil, and the oil pressure falls to c\napprox. 3.3 bar.\nHigh-pressure stage (3.3 bar)\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nOil pressure regulation when the oil pressure\n\n##### falls below 3.3 bar\n\nIf the oil pressure falls below 3.3 bar, for example because the engine speed is falling, then the same regulation is carried out as in the low pressure stage. The regulation at a constant pressure is a continual process in both pressure stages: \n\n- If the oil pressure is too low, the control port from the oil circuit to chamber 4 opens at the slide unit. The oil flowing in pushes it so that the pump gears intermesh further, more oil is pumped and the oil pressure increases. \n\n- If the oil pressure is too high, the return line from chamber 4 to the sump opens. The oil flowing back pushes the slide unit so that the pump gears do not intermesh as far, less oil is pumped and the oil pressure decreases. \n\n33\n\nEngine mechanics",
