    "text": "## Cooling system\n\nA dual-circuit cooling system is used for engine cooling in all EA211 engines. With this system, the coolant is channelled separately through the cylinder block and cylinder head at different temperatures. The temperature regulation is controlled by two thermostats in the thermostat housing. The respective coolant temperatures differ according to the engine.\n\n##### Special features of the charge air cooling system:\n\n##### Special features of the engine cooling system:\n\n- Cooling for the integrated exhaust manifold \n\n- Cross-flow cooling in the cylinder head for a more uniform temperature distribution \n\n   - Charge air cooling pump V188 \n\n   - Air-to-liquid charge air cooler in intake manifold \n\n- Thermostat housing with integrated coolant pump \n\n   - Cooling of the exhaust gas turbocharger \n\n- Coolant pump driven by the exhaust camshaft using a toothed belt \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nCoolant expansion tank Heat\nexchanger for heater\nLine up to the\nT piece until 05/2013 Auxiliary heater\nCoolant temperature sender\nG62\nCylinder head/\ncylinder block Thermostat housing\nwith\nintegrated coolant\nCharge air cooler Engine oil cooler Line from 06/2013\nCharge air cooling pump\nT piece V188\nCoolant temperature\nsender on the\nradiator outlet G83\nWater radiator for charge Radiator s511_238\nair cooling circuit\nThe charge-air cooling system needs to be bled after it is opened to ensure a correct cooling output.\nThe system is bled either by using the cooling system charge unit VAS 6096 or by using the guided\nfunction “Filling and bleeding cooling system”. Please note the instructions in ELSA.\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n36",
