    "text": "### Engine cooling system\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nThe dual-circuit cooling system for the engine pumps the coolant from a coolant pump integrated into the\nthermostat housing to the cylinder head and cylinder block.\nThe dual-circuit cooling system has the following\nadvantages:\nThe cylinder block warms up faster because the\ncoolant remains in the cylinder block until it\nreaches approx. 105 °C.\nLess friction in the crankshaft group due to the\nhigher temperature level in the cylinder block.\nBetter cooling of the combustion chambers due to\nthe lower temperature level in the cylinder head.\nThis ensures better filling with a lower knocking\ntendency.\ns511_026\nThermostat housing with Drive gear on the\nintegrated coolant pump exhaust camshaft\nThe thermostat housing is installed on the cylinder Thermostat 1\nhead on the gearbox side. The coolant pump has\nbeen integrated into the thermostat housing to\nproduce the most compact cooling system design\npossible. The coolant pump is driven by the exhaust\ncamshaft using a toothed belt.\nThermostat 2\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n##### Thermostat 1 for cylinder head\n\nIt opens at a temperature of 87 °C or above, and opens the path from the radiator to the coolant pump. It opens at a coolant temperature of 80 °C or above in the MPI engines.\n\n##### Thermostat 2 for cylinder block\n\nIt opens at a temperature of 105 °C or above, and opens the path for the warm coolant from the cylinder block to the radiator. \n\nThe entire coolant circuit is open. \n\n37\n\nEngine mechanics",
