    "text": "### Charge air cooling pump V188\n\nThe electronically control charge air cooling pump is bolted to the housing cover of the oil separator below the intake manifold. It is a component of a selfcontained cooling system. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\ns511_244\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n##### Task\n\nThe charge air cooling pump conveys coolant from the water radiator for charge air cooling circuit to the charge air cooler in the intake manifold and to the exhaust gas turbocharger. The pump is activated by a PWM signal by the engine control unit when required. Charge air cooling pump V188 It is always activated at maximum power. It is activated under the following conditions: - - briefly after each time the engine is started when the engine is running, every 120 seconds for - constantly above a torque requirement of approx. 10 seconds to avoid heat accumulation, above all 100 Nm in the exhaust gas turbocharger and, - - constantly from a charge air temperature of 50 °C depending on the engine map, for 0 - 480 in the intake manifold seconds after the engine is turned off to avoid - at temperature differences of less than 12 °C in overheating with formation of steam bubbles in the charge air before and after the charge air the exhaust gas turbocharger. cooler Effects upon failure The different faults which can occur on the charge air cooling pump have the following effects: Cause of fault Effect Electrical fault or mechanical fault - Entry in engine control unit event memory - Loss of power Open circuit in signal line - Entry in engine control unit event memory - Pump running at top speed Open circuit in a pump voltage supply wire - Entry in engine control unit event memory - The pump does not work - Loss of power olksw \n\nPlease note that, for example, a highly soiled water radiator for charge air cooling circuit or an incorrectly filled cooling system can result in an entry in event memory, which will require an examination of the charge air cooling system. \n\n\n\n56\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nActivating the charge air cooling pump\nControl electronics are installed in the pump. They allow the electric motor to be activated, and they monitor the\npump function. They transmit the actual state of the pump to the engine control unit by switching the PWM signal to\nearth at regular intervals.\nCharge air cooling pump “OK”\n2 V/div= 2 s/div\nDuring pump operation, the control electronics switch\nthe PWM signal from the engine control unit to earth\nfor 0.5 seconds in 10-second intervals. This tells the\nengine control unit that the pump is ready for\noperation.\ns511_262\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n##### Charge air cooling pump “not OK”\n\nIf the self-diagnosis detects a fault, caused for example by a blocked pump or a dry-running pump, the control electronics change the duration of the earth switching of the PWM signal depending on the cause of the fault. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n2 V/div= 2 s/div\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\ns511_261 \n\nIn the event of fault, attempts will be made at regular intervals to switch the charge air cooling pump back on. If this succeeds, the control electronics transmit the charge air cooling pump “OK” signal to the engine control unit again. \n\n57\n\nEngine management system",
