    "section_id": "auiliteraturtemplate_s0045",
    "page_start": 22,
    "page_end": 22,
    "text": "### **Cooling of the integral exhaust manifold**\n\nThe integral exhaust manifold facilitates rapid heating of the coolant and, therefore, is an integral part of the thermal management system. \n\nDuring the warm-up phase, heat is transferred to the coolant within a very short period of time. This heat is directly utilized to heat the engine and passenger compartment. Due to the reduced heat loss, the downstream components - oxygen sensor, turbocharger and catalytic converter - are able to reach their optimal operating temperature more quickly. \n\nThe system goes into cooling mode even after a short warm-up phase. This is necessary because the coolant in proximity to the integral exhaust manifold would otherwise boil off very quickly. For this reason, Engine Coolant Temperature Sensor G62 is installed at the hottest point in the cylinder head. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nIntake side\nMain water jacket\nUpper coolant tract\nLower coolant tract\nExhaust gas duct with flange\nconnecting to turbocharger\nExhaust side\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n606_032 \n\nAnimation showing the cylinder head and the integral exhaust manifold. \n\n22",
