    "text": "# **Traction control systems**\n\n##### **Pressure reduction**\n\nIf the tendency to lock up remains, the control system opens the ABS outlet valve with the ABS inlet valve closed. \n\nThe wheel cylinder's pressure can now be released into the pressure accumulator. As a result, the wheel is able to accelerate again. If the capacity of the pressure accumulator is not sufficient to eradicate the wheels' tendency to lock up, the ABS control system switches the return flow pump on to pump the brake fluid back into the reservoir counter to the brake pressure applied by the driver. This causes the pulsation in the brake pedal.\n\n##### IV(9): Closed\n\nOV(10): Open\n\n###### **ABS regulation**\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n\"reduce pressure\"\n1 - Foot brake depressed\n2 - Tandem brake\n6 - Return flow pump 2\nS374_306\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n##### **Pressure build-up**\n\nIf the wheel speed again exceeds a defined value, the control system closes the ABS outlet valve and opens the ABS inlet valve. The return flow pump continues to run as required. \n\nIf the lock up limit is reached again, the \"maintain pressure\", \"reduce pressure\" and \"build up pressure\" cycle is repeated until the braking process is completed or comparison of the wheel speeds shows that there is no further danger of the wheels' locking up. \n\nIV(9): Open OV(10): Closed \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n\"build up pressure\"\n1 - Foot brake depressed\n2 - Tandem brake\nmaster cylinder 1\n6 - Return flow pump 2\nS374_328\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n20",
