    "text": "# **Traction control systems**\n\n##### **Design**\n\nThe ESP system consists of: \n\n- The ABS/ESP control unit \n\n- The hydraulic unit with electronic return flow pump, \n\n- The four speed sensors, \n\n- The brake pressure sender, \n\nTo a large extent, the ESP system makes use of the ABS and TCS components. A control unit with corresponding software and a hydraulic unit with return flow pump are available to regulate the brake pressures. The hydraulic unit must be designed for 4-wheel TCS. \n\n- The TCS and ESP button, \n\n- The brake light switch, \n\n- The brake system warning lamp, \n\n- The ABS warning lamp, \n\n- The ESP and TCS warning lamp, \n\n- The ESP sensor unit and \n\n- The steering angle sender \n\nand in certain vehicles \n\nWarning lamps in the dash panel insert are used to inform the driver of control interventions which are taking place and the status of the ESP system. A button in the dash panel can be used to deactivate the ESP/TCS function. In certain vehicles, e.g. the Tiguan, only the TCS function is deactivated. Other brake systems, such as e.g. ABS, remain active even when the ESP function is deactivated. \n\n- An active brake servo or \n\n- A pre-charging pump. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nABS warning lamp\nSteering angle sender\nESP and TCS warning lamp\nBrake system warning lamp Speed sensor\nTCS and ESP button\nTandem brake\nS374_125\nESP sensor unit with\nthe lateral acceleration sender, the yaw rate sender and the\nControl unit and longitudinal acceleration sender.\nhydraulic unit with (Longitudinal acceleration sender in 4-Motion vehicles and\nreturn flow pump Brake light vehicles with electromechanical parking brake)\nBrake pressure sender switch\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n42\n\nThe system's sensors are sub-divided into the senders for registering the driver's command and those for sensing the vehicle behaviour. \n\nThe driver's command is registered by: \n\n- The steering angle sender, \n\n- Information from the engine control unit, \n\nThe actual vehicle behaviour is registered by: \n\n   - The speed sensors on all four wheels, \n\n   - A sender for registering longitudinal and lateral acceleration, \n\n      - A sender for sensing the yaw rate and \n\n      - A sender for registering the current brake pressure. \n\n- The brake light switch, \n\n- The brake pedal switch and \n\n- The brake pressure sender. \n\nThe steering angle represents the driver's direction command and actuation of the brake pedals represents the command to brake or stop. The brake pressure sender additionally provides information on the intensity of the desired braking. \n\nThe speed sensor signals are used to determine the four wheels' drive and brake slip. \n\nThe signals from the transverse acceleration sender, the longitudinal acceleration sender and the yaw rate sender provide information on the vehicle's longitudinal and transverse dynamics. The brake pressure sender registers the actual pressure in the brake system. \n\nIn vehicles with automatic gearboxes, a link to the gearbox management system is added in order, firstly, to register the current gear selection and secondly to enable the gearbox to be shifted independently in the event of ESP control intervention. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nDriver command\nSteering angle sender\nDirection of travel Engine control unit Nominal vehicle\nDesired speed Brake light switch and brake pedal behaviour\nDesired deceleration switch\nBrake pressure sender\nESP intervention\nwhen the vehicle does\nnot behave as\nVehicle behaviour commanded by the\nBrake pressure sender\ndriver\nAcceleration\nDeceleration Longitudinal / transverse Actual vehicle\nacceleration sender\nYaw rate (yawing moment) behaviour\nYaw rate sender\nLongitudinal and transverse\nGearbox control unit\nacceleration\nS374_130\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nThe ABS/ESP control unit uses the data from the driver's command to calculate the nominal vehicle behaviour and the actual vehicle behaviour from the vehicle's actual movements. By comparing the two values, the ESP software detects a critical driving situation, and the necessary control interventions are introduced. \n\n43",
