    "text": "#### Example: Misfiring\n\nUnder all driving conditions the system checkswhether: \n\n1. The number of misfires is high enough to damage the catalytic converter, \n\nIf the fault causes a deterioration in emission quality, the self-diagnosis fault warning lamp (MIL) must indicate the fault by a steady light after fulfilling the relevant storage and switch-on conditions (immediately, after 2 driving cycles). \n\n2. the number of misfires causes the emission values to deteriorate by a factor of 1.5. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nIf the 1st condition is met, the self-diagnosis fault\nwarning lamp (MIL) must flash once a second.\n175_014\nFlashing rate\n1/sec\nIf the 2nd condition is met, then a fault is stored\nat the end of the first driving cycle but the self-\ndiagnosis fault warning lamp (MIL) does not\ncome on.\n175_015\nIf the fault continues to exist until the end of the\nsecond driving cycle, the fault warning lamp\nmust be steady on.\nSteady light\n175_016\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nIf the 1st condition is met, the self-diagnosis fault warning lamp (MIL) must flash once a second. \n\n\n\n13\n\nOverview of OBD-II (Gasoline Engine) On-Board Diagnosis The diagnosis fault codes are standardized to The third character indicates the module in which SAE and must be used in a harmonized fashion the fault occurs: by all manufacturers. The fault code always consists of a five-character Px1xx Fuel and air metering alphanumeric value, e.g. P0112. Px2xx Fuel and air metering Px3xx Ignition system The first character is a letter. It identifies the Px4xx Additional emission control system type: Px5xx Cruise and idle speed control Px6xx Computer and output signals Pxxxx for drive Px7xx Transmission Bxxxx for body Cxxxx for suspension and The fourth and fifth characters contain the Uxxxx for future systems component/system IDs. Only P-codes are required for OBD II. When carrying out a diagnosis, you can initiate The second character identifies the standard different diagnosis functions by entering different code. address words. olksw \n\n\n\n- P0xxx Freely selectable fault codes defined in accordance with SAE which can be used by the diagnosis system and contain specified fault texts (from model year 2000: P0xxx and P2xxx). \n\n- P1xxx Additional freely selectable fault codes affecting exhaust emissions which are offered by the manufacturer and contain no specified fault texts but must be registered with lawmakers (from model year 2000: P1xxx and P3xxx) \n\nThe Scan Tool Mode is started by entering the key \"33\". \n\nIt includes all functions stipulated for a generic Scan Tool by lawmakers within the framework of the OBD. In this context, individual physical data (e.g. Lambda probe data) can be exported. \n\nWorkshops with universal diagnosis units such as the VAG 1551/1552 can optimize troubleshooting by accessing all key engine data by entering the key \"01\". In the Bosch Motronic, the Readiness Code can also be generated by taking the vehicle out on a short trip. \n\n\n\nIf no faults are stored in the fault memory, do not erase the fault memory unnecessarily, otherwise this will reset the Readiness Code. \n\n14\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n1. Connect diagnosis system 9. Entry \"Q\".\nto diagnosis interface. Acknowledge entry.\nSwitch on unit.\n10. The fault messages stored\n2. Ignition \"On\". in the fault memory are\nprinted in alphanumeric\ntext.\n11. Entry \"06\"\n3. Self-diagnosis fault for\nwarning lamp (MIL)\nEnd of Output.\nindicates faults.\n12. Entry \"Q\".\n4. Entry \"1\" Acknowledge entry.\nfor rapid data transfer.\n13. Remedy fault.\n5. Entry \"01\".\nAddress word for\nengine electronics.\n14. After fault correction,\n6. Entry \"Q\". erase fault memory and\nAcknowledge entry. generate Readiness Code\nby short trip.\n(Motronic M5.9.2).\n175_903-\n175_910\n7. Entry \"Print\".\nSwitches printing\nmechanism on.\n8. Entry \"02\"\nfor Interrogate Fault\nMemory\n15\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nOverview of OBD-II (Gasoline Engine) \n\nThe display on the diagnosis system in OBD II Scan Tool Mode \n\nThis display appears after you enter \"1\" (for rapid data transfer) followed by \"33\" (for activate Scan Tool function). \n\n\n\nOBD II Scan Tool \n\nSelect Mode 1.. 2.. 3.. 4.. 5.. 6.. 7.. 8.. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nFor example, the following display appears after you select Mode 1.\nThis display contains various display fields which display the diagnosis data.\nFrom here, you can select the various PIDs (e.g. PID 5 = engine temperature, PID 16 = air mass flow rate).\nMode display\n7 modes are\nParameter Name of module\nselectable\n(41 - 47) identification Module 10\nPID1 = Motronic control unit\ne.g. 41\n= Fault code Module 1a\n= Diagnosis data\ntransmitted P0xxx/P1xxx = Transmission control unit\nMode 41 PID1 Module 10\n00000000 000001 1 1 01 1 01 1 0 1 00000000\nDisplay field 1 Display field 2 Display field 3 Display field 4\nNumber of stored ongoing diagnosis Displays whether Indicates whether the Readiness\nfaults; (e.g. misfiring components are Code was set.\nself-diagnosis fault recognition) supported by the\nwarning lamp (MIL) diagnosis.\nOn/Off\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\nThe readiness display \"00000000\" only indicates that all individual diagnosis affecting exhaust emissions were conducted in accordance with regulations. A \"0\" is set to denote a completed individual diagnosis even if a fault was detected and stored. \n\n16",
