    "text": "#### Requirement\n\nAn aged or defective primary catalytic converter probe prevents optimum adjustment of the air-fuel mixture and leads to a deterioration in the vehicle's emission and performance data. \n\nThis must therefore be detected by the engine management system after fulfilling the fault conditions, stored as a fault and indicated. \n\n27\n\nSystem Components (Gasoline Engine) <mark>Lambda control</mark> OBD II checks with regard to lambda control: G response/aging characteristics G voltage applied to lambda probes G probe heater Lambda probe aging diagnosis Aging or poisoning can adversely affect the A change in reaction time can be acquired, response of a lambda probe. A deterioration can stored and displayed but cannot be compenbecome noticeable by an increase in probe sated for. reaction time (period duration) or a shift in the probe voltage curve (probe shift). Both situations In the Motronic M5.9.2, the shift in the voltage lead to a reduction in the λ window and to curve is corrected within a defined framework by poorer exhaust gas conversion in the catalytic means of a second closed control loop converter. (adaption). olksw",
