    "text": "#### Differences with OBD-II (gasoline engine)\n\nIn parallel to pollution control and monitoring in the gasoline engine, the diesel engine also has low-pollution components. These components are monitored via OBD-II. \n\nOBD-II (Diesel) monitors the following components and systems: \n\n- G Misfire detection system \n\n- G Exhaust gas recirculation \n\n- G Injection commencement control \n\n- G Charge pressure control \n\nThe objectives and requirements relating to OBD-II (diesel) are identical to those for the OBD-II (gasoline engine), with the exception that different components are monitored due to the different combustion processes which take place. \n\n- G CAN bus \n\n- G Diesel direct injection system control unit \n\n- G All sensors and actuators relevant to exhaust emissions which are connected to the control unit \n\n- G Automatic transmission \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nOBD II\nSoot\nparticulates\n175_152\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n60",
