    "text": "#### Emission limits for diesel vehicles\n\nIn addition to the previously mentioned pollutant emissions of gasoline vehicles, a further limit value must be observed in the case of diesel vehicles – the amount of soot particulates (PM). \n\nWe will illustrate this by quoting two examples of emission limits. Given that different test methods are used, the values cannot be compared with one another. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nG The limit values for passenger cars which are G The emission limits currently applicable in the\napproved for max. 12 persons applicable in Federal Republic of Germany comply with the\nthe U.S. Federal State of California with effect D3 standard.\nfrom model year 1999.\nThe limit values conform to the TIER1 approval\nlevel.\nLimit value g/km\nPollutant Durability MY ‘99\n2.4\n[mi] [g/mi]\n2.2\n50,000 0.25 2.0\nNMHC\n100,000 0.31 1.8\n1.6\n50,000 3.4 1.4\n100,000 4.2 1.2\nNOx 0.60 0.56\n0.6 0.50\n100,000 1.0\n50,000 0.08 0.2\n0.05\nParticulates\nCO HC + NOx PM\n175_044\n175_156\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\nNMHC (Non-Methane Hydrocarbon) are hydrocarbons which do not contain any methane fractions. \n\n61\n\nOverview of OBD-II (Diesel)",
