    "text": "### The oxidation catalytic converter\n\nIn diesel engines, it is not possible to use a 3-way catalytic converter as in the gasoline engine. The reason for this is the excess air which is required to burn the fuel. As a result, the exhaust gases contain a high oxygen concentration which prevents the use of 3-way catalytic converters. As the name already suggests, the oxidation catalytic converter can only transform pollutants CO2 CO2 H2O in the exhaust gases by oxidizing them. This means that the nitrogen oxides (NOx) cannot be converted by reduction as in the gasoline engine. The exhaust gas recirculation system was introduced nevertheless to limit the emission of nitrogen oxides. CO HC PM Catalyst layer 175_180 The design of the oxidation catalytic converter is very similar to the design of the 3-way catalytic converter, with the exception that the lambda probes are missing. In the oxidation catalytic converter, the exhaust gas must also flow through small ducts and, as a result, it passes over the active catalyst layer. olksw \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n175_182\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n66",
