    "text": "#### Commencement of injection valve N108\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nThe diesel direct injection system control unit\ncomputes the required point of commencement\nof injection and activates the commencement of\ninjection valve accordingly. The valve converts\nthe input signal to a control pressure which acts\non the injection timing piston within the distribu-\ntor injection pump.\nElectrical circuit Effects of failure\nThe injection commencement control fails. The\ncommencement of injection is controlled by\nmeans of a characteristic curve stored in the con-\nN108 trol unit.\n+ 175_202\n175_194\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nCommencement of injection valve N108\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n69\n\nSystem Components (Diesel) \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nThe exhaust gas recirculation system\n1 Diesel direct 3\ninjection system control unit\n(with integrated altitude sender)\n2 Exhaust gas recirculation valve\n3 EGR valve 4\n4 Air-mass flow meter\n5 Catalytic converter\n175_060\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nThe direct injection process operates at high combustion temperatures and using high oxygen components which promote the formation of nitrogen oxides (NOx). However, the resulting nitrogen oxides cannot be reduced by the oxidation catalytic converter. Their production must be reduced by using an exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) system. \n\ncontrolled depending on load conditions through the controlled addition of exhaust gas. \n\nThe exhaust gas recirculation rate is limited by the rise in hydrocarbon (HC), carbon dioxide (CO) and particle emissions. \n\nIf a certain amount of exhaust gas is added to the fuel-air mixture, the combustion temperature is reduced, the oxygen content in the combustion chamber is lowered and NOx emission is reduced. The emission characteristics can be \n\n70\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nThe OBD II checks:\nG the opening and closing functions of the G Electrical function of the exhaust gas\nEGR valve by the air-mass flow meter recirculation valve, altitude sender and\nair-mass flow meter\nProcedure:\nExhaust gas recirculation inactive The function of the exhaust gas recirculation\nsystem is tested by the diesel direction injection\nsystem control unit (EDC 15V) by means of the\nair-mass flow meter. The air mass flow is moni-\ntored when exhaust gas is fed in and compared\nwith setpoints in the control unit making\nAmbient air\nallowance for the signal generated by the\naltitude sender.\ndbyVolkswage Gdoesnotgu\nse ara\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n##### Exhaust gas recirculation active\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nExhaust gas\n175_185 Ambient air\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nThe basic principle of the function check requires that the air mass flow rate (ambient air) during exhaust gas feed be less than when the exhaust gas recirculation system is switched off. \n\n\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nEGR\nQLM\n175_183\nQLM = air mass flow, t = time\n1 = signal from integrated altitude sender\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nEGR\nNOK\nQLM\n175_184\na = Vacuum\nb = Atmospheric pressure\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\n71\n\nSystem Components (Diesel) Exhaust gas recirculation valve N18 This valve converts the signals generated by the diesel direct injection system control unit to a control pressure. For this purpose the exhaust gas recirculation valve is supplied with partial pressure by the engine and transfers this to the EGR valve if the control unit generates a corresponding signal. The pulse duty factor of the control signal defines the magnitude of the exhaust gas recirculation rate. 175_186 Effects of failure Electrical circuit If the valve fails, the exhaust gas recirculation system is shut down. N18 + 175_202 olksw",
