    "text": "### Charge pressure control\n\nTo optimize performance making allowance for exhaust emissions, the charge pressure also has to be regulated and monitored continuously. The charge pressure is adapted to the various driving situations so that a calculated air mass is always available for combustion. \n\nTo control the charge pressure, the diesel direct injection system control unit requires the signals generated by the engine speed sender, intake pipe temperature sender, intake manifold pressure sender, accelerator pedal position sender and altitude sender. The last is integrated in the control unit. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\nUsing these signals, the control unit calculates\nthe necessary nominal charge pressure and\nregulates the pulse duty factor for the charge\npressure control solenoid valve.\nThe OBD II checks: Procedure:\nG The opening and closing functions of the The diesel direct injection system control unit\ncharge pressure control solenoid valve by the compares the signal generated by the intake\nintake manifold pressure sender manifold pressure sender with the calculated\nG Electrical function and signal plausibility of setpoints. These setpoint are defined by means of\nthe charge pressure control sender and a characteristic curve and the input signal.\nsolenoid valve\nLegend: 7\n1 Diesel direct A\ninjection system control unit\n2 Intake manifold pressure and\nintake pipe temperature 6\nrofoff\namm\nmm oititt\noc nininn\nhtt\net sii\nodd\nro ucc\nfg emm\nniy .tntnn\npo oCC\nCt. ypp\nhg girirr\niry thh\npo ybb\ncy oVV\nbdetcetorP .GAnegawsklGAnegawsklAnegawsklnegawskl\n<!-- End of picture text -->\n\nThe diesel direct injection system control unit compares the signal generated by the intake manifold pressure sender with the calculated setpoints. These setpoint are defined by means of a characteristic curve and the input signal. \n\n\n\n<!-- Start of picture text -->\n6\n2 1\n8\n175_199\nrofoff\namm\noititt\nnininn\nhtt\nsii\nodd\n